\emph{3.2. Other assumptions}

A large array of complexity assumptions have been proposed so far in
pairing groups; see {[}12{]} for a listing of such assumptions. Most of
these assumptions come in two flavors --- computational and decisional
as in the case of BDH. Many of these assumptions were initially
introduced in the symmetric setting to be generalized later in the
asymmetric setting. As we have noted, some authors {[}37,19,18{]} also
used the notion of relativized assumption in the Type 3 setting ---
assuming that the problem remains hard even when the adversary is given
oracle access to \emph{ψ}.

Their apparent diversity notwithstanding, the complexity assumptions in
pairing groups can be broadly classified into two categories.

\begin{quote}
(i) Assumptions where the problem does not explicitly involve any
element from G\emph{T} --- examples are co-DHP, Linear, Strong DH,
Hidden SDH, and Poly-SDH.
\end{quote}

(ii) Assumptions where the problem involves a pairing computation and
hence element of G\emph{T} --- examples are BDH, Bilinear DH Inversion,
and Bilinear DH Exponent.

Given a hardness assumption in the Type 2 setting, our primary concern
here is to formulate its natural counterpart in Type 3. Note that if one
is given some element \emph{g}′if the input to problem P -2 includes
\emph{g}′case for Type 3 as \emph{ψ} is not known in that setting.
Hence, as in the case of BDH and co-DHP, we insist that both \emph{gz} 2
\emph{z} then that implies that \emph{gz z} ∈G′2, then one can easily
obtain \emph{gz} 1∈G1 is also part of the input. However, that is not
the 1= \emph{ψ(g}′2 \emph{z)} without knowing \emph{z}. So

1and \emph{gz} be included in the input to P -3 in order to make it the
natural counterpart of P -2 in the Type 3 setting. For an assumption in
Category (i) above, we can then use the argument of Lemma 2 of {[}16{]}
(which establishes the equivalence of co-DHP and co-DHP*) to show that P
-3 is equivalent to P -2, while the argument put forth in Lemma 3 above
for the case of BDH can be readily adapted to show equivalence between P
-2 and P -3 for an assumption in Category (ii).

\textbf{Remark 3} (\emph{Weakening Assumptions})\textbf{.} For some
cryptographic protocols in Type 3, it might be possible to drop one or
more elements of G1 from the statement of P -3 without affecting the
reduction. For example, BF-IBE-3 can be proven secure under BDH-3b (see
Section 4.1.1). However, we do not know any protocol that can be proven
secure under the seemingly weaker

assumption BDH-3c. Note that the input to BDH-3c includes only
\emph{gα}1∈G1 along with \emph{gβ}2\emph{, gγ}2∈G2, whereas all known
security reductions based on BDH in the asymmetric setting require at
least one of \emph{gβ}1or \emph{gγ}1as part of the problem input. The
situation is similar for the `\,`weaker statement'\,' of the Linear
assumption in Type 3 as stated in {[}12{]} (also attributed to
Galbraith). In fact an interesting open question is to what extent one
can prune P -3 and still use it in the security reduction of a natural
cryptographic problem.

\textbf{4. Protocols in the asymmetric setting}

We revisit some existing pairing-based protocols in the asymmetric
setting. Some of these protocols employ the isomorphism \emph{ψ} in the
protocol itself and some others only in the security reduction. The
purpose of this investigation is twofold --- to determine the exact role
played by \emph{ψ} in the functionality and security of these protocols
and then to investigate whether it is possible to avoid the use of
\emph{ψ} altogether.

\emph{4.1. Protocols where ψ is used in the security argument only}

Several authors have used \emph{ψ} to argue the security of
pairing-based protocols in the asymmetric settings --- these protocols
include signature {[}10,5,7{]}, encryption {[}8,18{]}, and key agreement
{[}19{]} schemes.

The case of the BLS signature scheme has already been discussed in
detail in {[}16{]}. Several other signature schemes also use \emph{ψ} in
the security reduction including Boneh--Boyen short signature scheme
{[}5{]}, the Boneh--Boyen--Shacham short group signature scheme {[}7{]},
and the Delerablé-Pointcheval anonymous short group signature {[}15{]}.
In a later version {[}6{]} Boneh and Boyen modified the security
argument of their short signature scheme in {[}5{]} to remove the
necessity of \emph{ψ}; the same argument can also be used for the
Boneh--Boyen--Shacham short group signature scheme.

We would like to argue in general that it is possible to avoid \emph{ψ}
in the security argument. As illustrative examples we consider the case
of BF-IBE and an identity-based key agreement protocol. Our arguments
are quite general and are applicable to other protocols.

\emph{4.1.1. Boneh--Franklin IBE}\\
For simplicity we focus on the basic version of the BF-IBE protocol
(called BasicIdent in {[}8{]}). This protocol was originally described
in the symmetric setting but can also be implemented in the asymmetric
setting {[}26,37{]}. In contrast to previous findings, our study
indicates that Type 3 is indeed a better choice than Type 2 for BF-IBE
taking into account functionality, security and efficiency.

